{"verdict":"approved","issues":[{"severity":"minor","file":"src/services/contentstack.ts","line":79,"description":"The fallback string \"api.contentstack.io\" is a hardcoded endpoint URL. The review criteria require endpoint URLs to use named constants. While src/config/constants.ts does not exist in this project, a constant could be added to src/constants/contentstack.ts (e.g. DEFAULT_CONTENTSTACK_API_HOST) and referenced here instead of inlining the string.","suggestedFix":"Add `export const DEFAULT_CONTENTSTACK_API_HOST = \"api.contentstack.io\";` to src/constants/contentstack.ts and reference it in the live_preview host fallback."},{"severity":"minor","file":"src/pages/_app.tsx","line":96,"description":"The `onEntryChange` callback is registered as async, but there is no guarantee that @contentstack/live-preview-utils awaits the returned Promise. If the SDK fires another entry change before the previous async re-fetch completes, a race condition could cause stale preview data to overwrite newer data. The current `setLivePreviewContent((prev) => ({ ...prev, [contextKey]: [entry] }))` uses the functional updater form which mitigates state races, but the fetch itself could still resolve out of order.","suggestedFix":"Consider adding a request-id or timestamp guard to discard stale fetch results, or debounce the callback. This is low-risk for a preview feature and does not block merge."}],"summary":"The implementation correctly addresses the prescribed root cause: (1) live_preview block added to the Stack options in contentstack.ts with CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN from env; (2) ContentstackLivePreview initialized in _app.tsx with onEntryChange callback that re-fetches draft entries using the existing getSingleEntry/getEntry helpers (proper reuse, no hand-rolled fetch logic); (3) IContentstackContext extended with livePreviewContent field to carry reactive state; (4) [[...slug]].tsx correctly merges livePreviewContent over server-side props before providing to ContentstackContext.Provider. The implementer deviated from the plan by using management_token instead of preview_token, but provided a thorough justification comment explaining that the installed SDK v3.17.2 consumes management_token, not preview_token — this is a correct adaptation to the real SDK behavior. The implementer also touched [[...slug]].tsx (not named in the plan) because the plan incorrectly located the ContentstackContext.Provider in _app.tsx when it actually lives in [[...slug]].tsx — this was a necessary correction. When no preview signal is active, livePreviewContent is null and the page renders published content identically (no regression). Errors in the onEntryChange callback are caught and logged, preventing preview failures from crashing the page. No secrets are hardcoded — CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN is read from process.env. No path traversal or Express security concerns apply. The test file (shipped by the pipeline, not the implementer) verifies live_preview wiring, published content regression, successive draft changes, and error propagation. All acceptance criteria are demonstrably met by the implementation."}
